# Anhydrous proton conduction at 150 °C in a crystalline metal-organic framework

## abstract
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are particularly exciting materials that couple porosity, diversity and crystallinity. But although they have been investigated for a wide range of applications, MOF chemistry focuses almost exclusively on properties intrinsic to the empty frameworks;the use of guest molecules to control functions has been essentially unexamined. Here we report Na-3(2,4,6-trihydroxy-1,3,5-benzenetrisulfonate) (named beta-PCMOF2), a MOF that conducts protons in regular one-dimensional pores lined with sulfonate groups. Proton conduction in beta-PCMOF2 was modulated by the controlled loading of 1H-1,2,4-triazole (Tz) guests within the pores and reached 5x10(-4) S cm(-1) at 150 degrees C in anhydrous H-2, as confirmed by electrical measurements in H-2 and D-2, and by solid-state NMR spectroscopy. To confirm its potential as a gas separator membrane, the partially loaded MOF (beta-PCMOF2(Tz)(0.45)) was also incorporated into a H-2/air membrane electrode assembly. The resulting membrane proved to be gas tight, and gave an open circuit voltage of 1.18 V at 100 degrees C.

## Anhydrous proton conduction at 150 8C in a crystalline metal–organic framework
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are particularly exciting materials that couple porosity, diversity and crystallinity. But although they have been investigated for a wide range of applications, MOF chemistry focuses almost exclusively on properties intrinsic to the empty frameworks; the use of guest molecules to control functions has been essentially unexamined. Here we report $N a_{3}(2,4,6$ -trihydroxy-1,3,5-benzenetrisulfonate) (named $\mathbf{\beta}_{\mathbf{\beta}}$ -PCMOF2), a MOF that conducts protons in regular one-dimensional pores lined with sulfonate groups. Proton conduction in $\mathbf{\beta}_{\mathbf{\beta}}$ -PCMOF2 was modulated by the controlled loading of 1H-1,2,4-triazole $\mathbf{(\bar{\Pi}z)}$ guests within the pores and reached $\bar{\mathsf{\pmb{5}}}\times\mathsf{10}^{-4}\mathsf{\pmb{\mathsf{S}}}\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ at $\yen02$ in anhydrous $H_{2},$ as confirmed by electrical measurements in $\Hat{\mathbf{H}}_{2}$ and $\ D_{2},$ and by solid-state NMR spectroscopy. To confirm its potential as a gas separator membrane, the partially loaded MOF $(\beta\mathrm{-}\mathsf{P C M O F}2(\mathbb{T}\mathbf{z})_{0.45})$ was also incorporated into a $\left|\mathsf{H}_{2}\right/$ air membrane electrode assembly. The resulting membrane proved to be gas tight, and gave an open circuit voltage of 1.18 $\pmb{\ v}$ at $100^{\circ}C$ .
M materials characterized by tunable pores permeating etal organic frameworks (MOFs) are breakthrough regular molecular scaffoldings1,2. These inorganic solids couple the variety of potential ligands from coordination chemistry with the topological diversity of solid-state chemistry. To date, most research on MOFs has focused on gas storage applications3, with particular interest for hydrogen4,5, carbon dioxide6,7 and methane8. These solids are noted for their crystallinity, which enables molecular-level structure determination by X-ray techniques. Transport phenomena, such as ion conduction, are more widely associated with disordered structures as movement of components in a solid typically disfavors crystallinity and, to date, studies on transport properties in crystalline MOF-type materials are rare9–11.
A phenomenon increasingly evidenced in coordination polymer materials is their ability to undergo reversible structural changes in the solid state in response to guest molecules, temperature or pressure12–15. For porous network solids, this point is itself remarkable as the solid must also be sufficiently stable to sustain pores. MOFs have shown the ability to reconcile stability and structural dynamics, and often achieve this with retention of crystallinity. Our team has been pursuing organosulfonate MOFs as the sulfonate group imparts ‘adaptable’ coordinative properties16 that are often manifested as reversible solid-state dynamics. Given that, and the well-established role of macromolecular sulfonic acid polymers as proton conductors17–20, we undertook a study of sulfonated MOFs as proton-conducting solids.
From the perspective of synthesizing new materials, there are several potential advantages to this approach. The open framework structure of MOFs could allow for controlled guest loading with non-volatile proton carriers, enabling proton conduction over $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . Being in the microporous realm, the open structure could be blocked with guest molecules to prevent hydrogen fuel crossover in a fuel cell configuration. From a design perspective, owing to the crystallinity of MOFs, molecular level insights into transport phenomena could be gained. Coupling this with the modularity of
MOF synthesis could open the door to a wide range of potentially better ion conductors. Here, we report a crystalline sulfonate coordination material, $\upbeta$ -PCMOF2 (proton conducting metal organic framework 2) with channels that can be loaded with amphoteric heterocycles to exhibit proton conduction under anhydrous $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . This is a new domain for MOF materials and also a different approach to proton-conducting solids21. The results are enabled by not only the controlled loading of certain guests in a MOF, but also by the specific stoichiometries of that guest. As a step towards application, we show that loaded $\upbeta$ -PCMOF2 can function both as a proton conductor and gas separator in a membrane electrode assembly (MEA) and exhibit open circuit voltages (OCVs) over $1.1\mathrm{V}$ at $\mathrm{i}00^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for a $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -air fuel cell. This is significant as membrane materials that function above the boiling point of water are needed for widespread use of proton exchange membrane (PEM) and direct methanol fuel cells (DMFCs).
## Results
The trisodium salt of 2,4,6-trihydroxy-1,3,5-benzenetrisulfonate, $\mathrm{L,}$ can be prepared as a lower-temperature $\upalpha$ -phase (not discussed here) and a higher-temperature $\upbeta$ -phase. $\upbeta$ -PCMOF2, crystallizes in a honeycomb structure with one-dimensional pores lined with sulfonate groups. The L molecules are disordered because of rotation in the plane of the benzene ring; each part of Fig. 1 represents one position of the molecule. The X-ray structure shows hexagonal sheets of L molecules, in the crystallographic $a b$ plane, crosslinked in the third dimension by Na ions to form channels (Fig. 1a). The channels in $\upbeta$ -PCMOF2 have an 18-membered periphery with a diameter of 5.65(2)–5.91(2) Å measured between Na ions and the transannular O atoms. The $\mathrm{Na^{+}}$ ions are eight-coordinate and ligated by two hydroxyl $(\mathrm{Na1-O6}=2.669(2)\mathrm{-}2\mathrm{\bar{.}676}(2)\mathrm{\:\AA})$ and six sulfonate oxygen atoms $(\mathrm{Na1-O}=2.350(2)\mathrm{-}2.798(2)\mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ . The sheets of ligands are packed efficiently with the nearest intermolecular C...C distance at 3.987(2) Å (Fig. 1b). Significantly, the pores are lined exclusively with oxygen atoms of sulfonate groups (Fig. 1c,d) to form a potential hydrogen-bond transfer pathway. Figure 2 shows powder X-ray data confirming that $\upbeta$ -PCMOF2 can be prepared as a matching bulk sample.
