For PEMs and direct methanol fuel cells, a major challenge is proton conduction above $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (see below)17–23. An approach to attain this goal is to replace water as the proton carrier with an amphoteric $N.$ -heterocycle, such as imidazole24,25. The imidazole/imidazolium pair can transfer protons via a Grotthus-type mechanism and are also a less volatile base–acid pair. As the channels in $\upbeta$ -PCMOF2, were sufficiently large to include such heterocycles, this approach was examined. Our results with 1H-1,2,4-triazole $(\mathrm{Tz})$ are presented here. In our studies, $\mathrm{Tz}$ was found to be a superior anhydrous proton transfer agent than imidazole, as has been put forth by $\mathrm{Liu}^{2\bar{6}}$ .
Impedance analyses show that the loaded $\mathsf{\beta}\mathrm{-PCMOF2(Tz)}_{x}$ samples are much better proton conductors than their unloaded, hydrated counterparts, and indeed function above $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The data also confirm a dependence of proton conductivity on $\mathrm{Tz}$ loading. Figure 3 shows Arrhenius plots from the impedance analysis of four samples of $\mathsf{\beta}_{\mathsf{B-P C M O F}2(\mathrm{Tz})_{x}}$ ( $\overset{\cdot}{x}=0$ , 0.30, 0.45 and 0.60). At $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the conductivity measured for $x=0.30$ , 0.45 and 0.60 Tz-loaded samples reached roughly $2\times10^{-4}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ , $5\times10^{-4}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and $4\times\mathrm{{\bar{10}^{-4}}~S~c m^{-1}}$ , respectively. Neither pure triazole nor pure $\upbeta$ -PCMOF2 show proton conductivity above $10^{-6}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . PXRD analyses confirmed the retention of the original structure after impedance analyses (Fig. 2).
In order to confirm that the observed electrical effects were due to proton conduction, and not electron or $\mathrm{Na^{+}}$ conduction, a.c. impedance experiments were performed in $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{D}_{2}$ , with and without gas diffusion electrodes. In Fig. 4, the a.c. impedance plots of PCMOF- $2(\mathrm{Tz})_{0.3}$ in anhydrous $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{D}_{2}$ , with gas diffusion electrodes, are shown. Interestingly, three clear semicircles due to bulk, grain-boundary and electrode polarization contributions were observed in both $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{D}_{2}$ . A noteworthy feature is the absence of the tail at low frequencies indicating the non-blocking nature of the electrolyte–electrode interfaces. Also, for $\mathrm{D}_{2}$ , the expected kinetic isotope effect was observed in that the bulk resistance was ${\sim}1.5$ times higher than $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , which is consistent with deuteron versus proton migration33.
As a critical proof-of-principle experiment, to further confirm the potential of a MOF as a membrane, a membrane–electrode assembly with $\mathsf{\beta}_{\mathsf{B-P C M O F}2(\mathrm{Tz})_{0.45}}$ was constructed and the electromotive force of the $\mathrm{H}_{2/}$ air cell was measured. The MEA was prepared by tying platinum wires to porous $\mathrm{Pt-C}$ electrodes. $\mathsf{\beta{-}P C M O F{2}(T z)_{0.45}}$ powder was pressed between the electrodes in a die, with the $\mathrm{Pt}$ wires away from the sample. A gas-tight connection, via a hollow ceramic tube, was made between the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ source and the anode. The other side of the MEA was left open to air as the cathode. Electromotive force measurements (Fig. 5) showed an open circuit voltage of $1.18{\mathrm{V}},$ stable for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . There was a decrease in the OCV value to 0.93 and $0.77\mathrm{V}$ at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ respectively, which was attributed to fuel crossover.
## Discussion
In Fig. 6, we compare the proton conduction of $[\mathsf{\beta-P C M O F2(T z)}_{0.3}]$ with well-known proton conductors reported in the literature, particularly focusing on the region over $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ Interestingly, the $\mathrm{Tz}$ -loaded PCMOF exhibits conductivity about four orders of magnitude higher than $\mathrm{Zr}$ -phosphate and $\upbeta$ -alumina-based ceramic proton conductors24. It is also three orders of magnitude lower than that of fully hydrated Nafion and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ -derived proton conductors24. However, the proton conductivity of Nafion and PBI$\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ (ref. 34) depend highly on humidity, whereas the loaded PCMOF shows proton conduction in anhydrous conditions. More importantly, one can pose the question for the other materials charted, ‘What are the options for rationally designing improvements?’ From a materials design perspective, the MOF approach offers a precisely known structure and ample options for systematic, modular variation to improve conductivity and robustness.
The demonstration of anhydrous proton conduction in a MOF is only a step towards applicability as a fuel cell membrane. Anhydrous proton conduction above $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is significant to fuel cell technologies as, for PEMFC and DMFC systems, efficiencies are improved by operation above $100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}^{17-23}$ . The reasons are multifold and include: enhancing electrode kinetics; decreasing CO poisoning of $\mathrm{\Pt}$ -based electrodes; management of only a single phase of water above $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ However, basic issues that could disqualify this electrolyte include: the ability to construct a dense membrane from the powder; inadequate PCMOF/electrode contact; partial electronic conductivity of the MOF; or catastrophic fuel crossover. The open circuit voltage experiment on the PCMOF-electrode assembly shows that none of these potential issues are overriding; a guest-loaded, proton-conducting MOF can function to separate $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ from the cathode half-cell and sustain a marked potential difference across the electrolytic cell at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . With respect to fuel crossover, the one-dimensional pores shown in Fig. 1 would appear to invite passage of fuel between half-cells. However, a key point for $\upbeta$ -PCMOF2 is that $\mathrm{Tz}$ sits in, and is only slightly smaller than, the pores ( $\mathrm{\DeltaTz}$ is $4.21\mathrm{\AA}$ across whereas the pores are $5.6\mathring{\mathrm{A}}_{\iota}^{\cdot}$ . The value of $1.18{\mathrm{~V~}}$ for an OCV is near the theoretical maximum, at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 1 atm pressure, for the formation of water vapour from the $\mathrm{H}_{2}{-}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ cell (1.16 V). Although it is higher than the theoretical value, the uncertainty in our OCV measurement itself is $\pm0.02\:\mathrm{V};$ , so it is within error. Further variation also comes from the temperature fluctuation in the tube furnace. The OCV value of $1.18\mathrm{V}$ at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is better than that of low temperature Nafion-based fuel cells35.
To contrast the MOF approach with other crystalline materials, solid acids possess crystalline architectures, which give insight into the mechanism of ion conduction, and have shown very good proton conductivity well above $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (ref. 36). Some of these materials can have both chemical and mechanical stability issues but there are options for varying inorganic components. However, the options to tune a solid acid framework by organic doping are not extensive as regular pores are not typically observed. Layered solid acids37,38, included in Fig. 6, will typically swell or exfoliate with hydration39. Comparison is also made to sulfonated three-dimensional mesoporous solids such as PFSA-alumina40. The mesopores in these solids enable covalent modification and hydrated samples to show good conductivity, but challenges with hydrogen fuel crossover can certainly be envisaged. The MOF approach merges the options to alter the inorganic components of the solid acid approach with the choice of organic components from the macromolecular approach in an open, crystalline architecture that can be made gastight by guest loading. With exact knowledge of the dimensions of a pore and the locations of hydrogen bond donors and acceptors, the pairing with the ideal proton transferring dopant could be made judiciously with sub-angstrom level insight.
Research on MOFs has mainly focused on gas storage4,8. Transport phenomena, including ion conduction, have been virtually ignored. To date, two families have been reported that shows ion conductivity. Cu thiooxamides that conduct protons in the $10^{-6}–10^{-5}\mathrm{Scm^{-i}}$ range below $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with hydration dependence9,10, and a recently reported ferrous oxalate hydrate that conducts on the order of $1\bar{0^{-3}}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at ambient conditions and $98\%$ relative humidity11. One MOF has been reported with sulfonated pores but no conductivity studies were performed41. The extrinsic doping of a MOF to give proton conduction above $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and its incorporation into a MEA to show a significant OCV are major advances. More generally, there are very few examples where an intrapore guest plays any role in the function of a $\mathrm{\bar{M}O F^{42,43}}$ . The guests may act as substrates for catalysis44–47 or sensing48,49, but typically they are simply removed to activate the pore for storage. This work represents a case where, not only is the nature of the guest fundamental to function, but the stoichiometry of loading is a determinant of performance.
We have presented a new approach to high-temperature proton conducting materials based on metal organic frameworks. PCMOFs can be designed based on molecular-level insights to structure and conduction pathways. The selection of host and guest components for PCMOFs is vast and improvements can be made based on lessons learned from both macromolecular and solid acid approaches to proton conduction. Further, PCMOFs could be used, as inorganic salts have, with complementary polymer supports to form proton conducting nanocomposites50. Ongoing work in our lab includes more robust PCMOFs with higher proton conductivity, optimal guest matching and mechanistic studies.
## Methods
Procedure for the synthesis of $\mathbf{\beta}_{\mathbf{\beta}}$ -PCMOF2. Anhydrous phloroglucinol $(8.053\ \mathrm{g},$ $63.86\mathrm{mmol}$ ) was dissolved in dry dimethyl carbonate $(200~\mathrm{ml})$ and the resulting yellow solution was cooled to $0{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under argon. Chlorosulfonic acid $(12.43~\mathrm{ml}\$ 186.8 mmol, 2.925 equiv.) was added dropwise over the course of two minutes with vigorous stirring, resulting in an orange coloured solution. The reaction was warmed to ambient temperature and allowed to proceed for $1.5\mathrm{h}$ under a steady flow of Ar before the system was sealed and stirred for an additional $22.5\mathrm{h}$ The solvent was then removed via rotary evaporation and the resulting viscous brown oil was dissolved in $150~\mathrm{ml}$ of water. Sodium bicarbonate was added to the solution until it reached a $\mathrm{\DeltapH}$ of 2.0. Addition of the aqueous solution to 4 l of acetone resulted in the formation of a thick white precipitate, which was isolated via vacuum filtration. This phase (a) was then converted to the $\upbeta$ -phase via heating in a bomb apparatus. As synthesized $\upalpha$ -PCMOF2 $(0.500\mathrm{g},1.16\mathrm{mmol})$ ) was placed into a $20~\mathrm{ml}$ teflon cup. To this, a mixture of water $(5~\mathrm{ml})$ , methanol $(1.5~\mathrm{ml})$ ) and acetone $(1.5~\mathrm{ml})$ was added. The teflon cup was then sealed in a hydrothermal bomb. The bomb was heated at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ and then cooled back to room temperature over a period of $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The resulting white precipitate was isolated via vacuum filtration.
General procedure for the synthesis of $\mathbf{\beta_{\beta-PCMOF}}2(\mathbf{T}\mathbf{z})_{\mathbf{0}.3}$ . Anhydrous phloroglucinol $(6.150~\mathrm{g}$ , 48.77 mmol) was dissolved in dry dimethyl carbonate $\mathrm{175ml}$ ) and the resulting yellow solution cooled to $0{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under Ar. Chlorosulfonic acid ( $9.50~\mathrm{ml}$ , $143~\mathrm{mmol}$ , 2.93 equiv.) was added dropwise over the course of two minutes with vigorous stirring, resulting in an orange coloured solution. The reaction was warmed to ambient temperature and allowed to proceed for $1.5\mathrm{h}$ under a steady flow of Ar before the system was sealed and stirred for an additional $22.5\mathrm{h}$ . The solvent was then removed via rotary evaporation and the resulting viscous brown oil dissolved in water to a concentration of $125\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{ml}^{-1}$ . 1,2,4-Triazole $(3.283\mathrm{g},47.54\mathrm{mmol},)$ ) was then added to the aqueous solution and allowed to stir for $24\mathrm{h}$ . Sodium bicarbonate $(5.992\ {\mathrm{g}},71.33\ {\mathrm{mmol}},1.5\$ equiv.) was added to the solution, which was then precipitated via addition to 3 l of acetone. The resulting thick white precipitate was isolated via vacuum filtration. Similarly, $\mathrm{\alpha_{\alpha}-P C M O F}2(\mathrm{Tz})_{0.45}$ was prepared by adding 1.35 equivalents (relative to ligand) of ${\mathrm{NaHCO}}_{3}$ , and $\upalpha\mathrm{-}\mathrm{PCMOF}2(\mathrm{Tz})_{0.6}$ was prepared by adding 1.2 equivalents (relative to ligand) of ${\mathrm{NaHCO}}_{3}$ , during preparation of the frameworks. The $\propto$ -phases were then quantitatively converted to $\upbeta$ -phase in a bomb apparatus. Approximately $_{2\mathrm{~g~}}$ of as synthesized ${\bf\alpha}_{\mathrm{\alpha-PCMOF}2(\mathrm{Tz})}$ was packed into a silicone tube, sealed and pressed under a hydrostatic pressure of $5,000\mathrm{kg}$ for $2~\mathrm{min}$ . The resulting pellet was removed from the silicone tube and placed into a $20~\mathrm{ml}$ teflon cup, along with a vial containing $0.5~\mathrm{ml}$ of water. The teflon cup was then sealed in a hydrothermal bomb and heated at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ .
Impedance spectroscopy. Impedance analysis was performed on pristine PCMOF powders without modification. All pellets were prepared by first grinding the samples into a homogeneous powder with a mortar and pestle. The powders $(\sim0.3\ \mathrm{g)}$ were then added to a standard $13~\mathrm{mm}$ die, sandwiched between two porous carbon electrodes (Sigracet, GDL 10 BB, $1.222\mathrm{cm}$ diameter) and pressed at $^{5,000}\mathrm{kg}$ for $2\mathrm{min}$ . All pellets were $13~\mathrm{mm}$ in diameter and ranged in thickness from 1.30 to $1.38~\mathrm{mm}$ . Measurements were performed using an impedance and gain-phase analyser (SI model 1260), 1 Hz–1 MHz, with a two-probe electrochemical cell equipped with platinum current collectors and an applied a.c. voltage of $\ensuremath{10-100}\ensuremath{\mathrm{mV}}$ (unless otherwise stated). Measurements were taken in the temperature range of 23 to $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in either an anhydrous $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{D}_{2}$ atmosphere. Conductivity was calculated using the following equation : $:\sigma=l/R_{\mathrm{s}}S,$ where $l$ and S are the thickness $\scriptstyle({\mathrm{cm}})$ and cross-sectional area $(\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ of the pellet respectively, and $\ensuremath{\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{s}}}$ , which was extracted directly from the impedance plots, is the bulk resistance of the sample $(\Omega)$ .
